<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Jumbe State of Nkhotakota</h1><p class="st-meta">1845 CE – 1894 CE (approx.) · capital Nkhotakota</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Swahili trading state on the western shore of Lake Malawi, a hub of the slave trade and of Islam in the region.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1845 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salim-bin-abdallah">Salim bin Abdallah</a>, a Swahili-Arab trader from the coast connected with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a>, settled at Nkhotakota on the western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>. Taking the title Jumbe, he made himself ruler over the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chewa">Chewa</a> and built the town into a trading base.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Successive Jumbes ran a commercial state that shipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and enslaved people by dhow across the lake towards <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa</a> and the coast; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a>, who visited in 1863, described the trade. Mosques and Koranic schools made Nkhotakota a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chewa">Chewa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Jumbe signed a treaty with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1889 and cooperated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> for a time, but in the mid-1890s the British removed the last Jumbe and absorbed Nkhotakota into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1845 CE – 1894 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.42%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1845 CE – 1894 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nkhotakota</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Salim bin Abdallah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili, Chichewa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jumbe of Kota Kota, Nkhotakota</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1870&amp;focus=jumbe-state-of-nkhotakota" aria-label="Jumbe State of Nkhotakota on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5621 2659 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jumbe State of Nkhotakota lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5941 2915L6086 2664M5941 2915L5964 3006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6086 2664h0M5964 3006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5941 2915h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5941 2915h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nkhotakota. <a href="/atlas?year=1870&amp;focus=jumbe-state-of-nkhotakota">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
